 so that is why it is physically and  mentally hard but we need to continue  is it possible to negotiate with a man  like vladimir putin i know you're not in  the room with him but he is most  definitely in charge  it is hard uh because  he tries to open up several verticals  which means that  legally when you negotiate with  on one topic  you constantly are fighting back in the  field  in addition uh he goes and fights you in  the media  so basically there are up to five six  different dimensions that you have to  fight  the decision is only in his head  so the only person who can stop this  bloodshed is vladimir putin  but at this stage as i said  every person who comes to negotiations  they have their own mandate  uh and  every person is mandated to negotiate  his own block  so at this stage it's hard but it  doesn't constitute that it's impossible  what is your mandate then  my job is to mediate uh to  architecturize  these negotiations  so that  they happen physically we don't stop  in being in contact with them  we have groups  that are represented by  ministry of defense general chiefs of  staff  minister of foreign affairs there are  humanitarian  issues but  this event of these negotiations we were  able to  evacuate up to 300 000 people from  six different regions 45 different  locations so  and now we're working on  possible evacuation  of people uh from mariupol so which  means that even though it's hard  but we save life so that's why we need  to be  continuing it  the evacuation from mariopol  particularly  difficult i know there have been so many  kind of  false starts if you like  hopes that it was going to happen  apparent agreements that fell apart  it is extremely hard to  agree on something with them why because  when  you negotiate you discuss different  categories of people children  wounded  women maybe kidnapped people  so uh when it comes to the  implementation there are always games  why because  they could  give you the  one checkpoint but they will be coming  to another checkpoint so there are many  peculiarities and  we take it seriously nevertheless we  understand that they don't keep promises  and that's why we sometimes ask the  international community international  partners to be able to  witness it why because they're twisting  the story in world media saying that  ukraine is responsible but i would like  to say that ukraine is trying to defend  its own territory  uh ukraine is trying to save as many  lives as possible so that is why we're  trying to evacuate  people wounded kidnapped  people that were  even children  just recently we brought back uh two  children that were forcibly taken to  a russian federation uh we know that  there are many  people killed and their  children forcibly taken to russian  federation but we're fighting for every  person and for  every part of our territory and for the  democracy so that is why we are  continuing these negotiations  you were one of the people i understand  it along with roman abramovich who had  symptoms of poisoning after one session  of peace talks can you tell me about  that and the effect it had on you  well i already  informed the media that i'm fine at the  moment but  it  happened maybe three four weeks ago  nevertheless uh my colleagues who are  more  into details whether it was a chemical  or  magnetic poisoning i'm not sure so  that's why i'm  not trying to tell more about it but  they're working on it  and soon i hope they will come up with a  result what was it  but at the moment i am fine and i'm  continuing  my job  can you help me understand or what your  understanding is of roman abramovich's  role in this he's one of russia's  richest oligarchs of course said to be  close to vladimir putin what is he doing  there  well uh he is  independent he is not part of the  russian delegation nevertheless he is a  mediator  he tries to mediate he tries since he is  not  from  [Music]  the state let's say affairs  so  sometimes there are issues that is hard  to discuss  so he goes to  the  russian part and discusses how we  perceive this issue  so  his role is mandated also  in  making  negotiations  continue  mainly he tries to focus on to the  humanitarian bloc  as i said like  those  kind of  exits or evacuation corridors  so  at this stage i cannot answer on behalf  of him but  he's involved in mediation as well  the west is of course providing more  lethal weapons more every day the  rhetoric too is ramping up the us  defense secretary says he wants russia  weakened russia's foreign minister says  the west is waging a proxy war against  russia and warns of provoking world war  iii  what do you read into all that and how  worried should the world be  well the world should definitely be  worried why because the war already  started so  every person who is not involved at this  stage should be  uh  should be thinking that it helps than  russia if it is not involved in  finishing the war on the  supply side we appreciate  canada's support for half a billion  support they provided and now they will  be providing uh some  apc's armored vehicles  we appreciate it  we appreciate canadian people for the  support  for the political and  diplomatic support that canada does  nevertheless world should be focused on  what ukraine needs in the fields  so when we talk a lot about potential  supply  uh it doesn't constitute that this  equipment has been brought to ukraine  has been brought to the fields to the  armed forces because uh we have uh  allegedly second largest uh army  fighting against us  and we don't have  uh  any equipment at this stage  to fight them back so that is why world  should be focusing whether we have it  rather than  potentially supplying it so if ukrainian  armed forces doesn't have it on the  field  it means that nothing is done  so at this stage what world could do is  to supply immediately  and bring it logistically to the fields  that could be a big support to us to our  armed forces and to our resistance  it sounds like you're telling me  promises have been made but not  necessarily not necessarily delivered on  well i would say that as a person who  is now  supervising some of the  issues related to this block  uh there are many uh  rules and regulations related to the  lethal and non-lethal supplies  uh  is it fortunate or unfortunate for  ukraine it's unfortunate because we need  it immediately but it has been regulated  a long time before us and to  get through these regulations it's very  hard because sometimes countries are  saying that we're able to supply but we  don't want to supply directly let's use  the third uh countries sometimes they  say  we can make a agreement but we cannot  make payment  sometimes they make payment but they  cannot make logistics directly so  basically speaking  uh most of the world is now concerned on  how they will be looking in  russian ice rather than supplying  ukrainian armed forces  with equipment  unfortunately unfortunately still many  countries are afraid  to supply directly to ukraine because  they will be in bad situation with  russian federation on diplomatic stance  or political stance and  is it a double standards i don't know  but  uh we're fighting not only for our own  independence but for the european and  global securities so we need the world  to know that  diplomacy and politics works until the  bombs  finished so at this stage we need to  fight back in the fields and we need  their support so they should not be  playing around  uh trying to  convince us that they will be in bad  situations with russia uh russians are  killing us so that is why  we want world to help us and bring the  equipment to the fields  is it do you think that canada has done  enough and has acted fast enough  canada is active uh canada is helping  diplomatically politically uh as i  already mentioned uh they already  supplied half a billion and they will be  supplying some apc's armament vehicles  nevertheless  the  speed is extremely important so we need  to supply as much equipment as we can  otherwise  this may uh  become a beginning of third world war it  is already  in progress but if we are  not going to defend ukraine it means  that  all the other countries  will be threatened by russia and we are  the only country at the moment able to  fight back  russian federation  this of course is not the first time  vladimir putin has invaded one of his  neighbors  it is hard to see any signs that he can  be trusted or that he's inclined to back  down or give anything up what are your  expectations of him  well our uh  major objective is to finish the war  by saving  all people  that we can  without  any risk to our territorial integrity  which means that we're going to fight  for our people for our territories for  democracy for the human rights  and it is our right to defend ourselves  so at this stage there is no much  of expectation but  what is happening now  every country should be re-evaluating  what it has done for the last 10 years  with this country because nowadays when  we see on our fields  tanks  aircrafts with so many  western parts supplied to russian  federation  and for the last eight years when the  world was saying don't supply defensive  weapons to  ukraine it makes us feel bad  so that is why we have to reevaluate  what is that what is being done now what  has been done before  to avoid this because today's bloodshed  has been taken the decisions has been  taken before so  my  concern is that there is no expectation  our major focus has to stop the war  and the world should be besides ukraine  helping to supply weapons helping  diplomatically politically and  financially with ukraine because as i  said we're not only fighting for our own  we are fighting for the democracy with  fighting for the human rights  and for the security for the global  security  mr omarov thank you so much for speaking  with us  and taking so much time and  we appreciate everything you're doing  thank you very much

